        "title": "Strange Attractors",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "A strange attractor is the geometric home of chaos in a continuous flow: a **bounded, invariant,\nattracting set** that is also **fractal** (non-integer dimension) and on which the dynamics are\n**chaotic** (positive Lyapunov exponent, SDIC). It is \"strange\" in its *geometry* (fractal) and\n\"chaotic\" in its *dynamics* (sensitive) — usually both at once. The Lorenz attractor is the founding\nexample, born from a drastic truncation of fluid convection; the Rössler attractor is the minimal,\nhand-buildable cartoon. This chapter is where Chapter 05's invariants (Lyapunov exponents) and\nChapter 07's geometry (fractal dimension) meet on actual objects."
    },
    {
      "id": "06-STRANGE-ATTRACTORS.md#002",
      "path": [
        "06-STRANGE-ATTRACTORS.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
